Platform: 13th Gen Core i9-13900T (Raptor Lake, 8 P-cores with SMT + 16 E-cores)
with its UHD 770 iGPU (0xa780) on i915 and two NVIDIA RTX Ada GPUs (0x27b0 RTX 4000
SFF Ada, 0x28b0 RTX 2000 Ada) on the nvidia driver, kernel 6.6, Docker 29 with
NVIDIA CDI devices registered but no nvidia runtime.

Source: CAPTURED from real hardware (the k3d-gpu development host) with
tests/fixtures/capture.sh; nothing under sysroot/ was edited by hand. First
capture 2026-10-16 (ids, drivers, nodes, firmware). Recaptured later on 2026-10-16
with the doctor --perf inputs added to capture.sh, run as root inside a throwaway
container (`docker run --privileged --network none -v /:/host:ro`, then
`capture.sh <out> /host`) so the root-only inputs are real: CPU 0 MSRs 0x19C,
0x1B1, 0x64F, 0x6A2, 0x982 (read through the msr driver) and the four SMBIOS
type 17 records (numeric fields only; the string set with locators, serials and
part numbers is dropped). /proc entries come from the host's procfs (host PID
namespace); the root mount from /proc/1/mounts. The cpuinfo family/model lines
and the i915 card0/engine/*/capabilities files come from a capture of the same
host (same day) after capture.sh learned to read them. Every other file outside
the doctor --perf inputs is byte-identical to the first capture.

Throttle counters, temperatures, uptime, meminfo and the MSR status/log bits are
a snapshot taken while a benchmark ran on the host, so the thermal section shows
active throttling.

env records that host's Docker state (`docker info` runtimes: io.containerd.runc.v2
runc; CDI ids nvidia.com/gpu=0, =1, =all; per-GPU UUID ids dropped) and that
nvidia-ctk and nvidia-container-runtime are installed. Device names: pci.ids 2026.10.15.

allocatable is what the device plugins advertise for two GPUs and one iGPU; it is
not read from a running cluster.
